+## Select for the artifact's reader
+
+Available context is not automatically material for the artifact. Write for the
+intended reader without assuming they followed the chat, debugging, or development
+session. Explain unfamiliar references when the audience needs them.
+Keep the artifact focused on its requested subjects. Removing irrelevant material
+does not call for a replacement sentence explaining why you removed or rejected it.
+
+For current-product documentation, describe the supported behavior, interfaces,
+requirements, and limits. Omit abandoned approaches, editing notes, refactoring
+narratives, and defenses of ordinary choices unless they help the reader understand,
+decide, use, troubleshoot, or maintain the system. A polished account of irrelevant
+history is still irrelevant.
+
+Let the document's purpose decide what history belongs. Keep consequential rationale,
+alternatives, chronology, uncertainty, and failed or invalidated results in records
+that need them: decision records, migration guides, incident reviews, changelogs,
+and evaluation reports. Do not strip evidence to make a record sound cleaner.
+
+When drawing on implementation notes, chat history, or mixed-purpose drafts, read
+[audience and artifact boundaries](references/editorial-judgment.md) for the relevance
+test, genre distinctions, and examples. Apply this selection before polishing prose.
 
